<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Influence of Number of Visual Tokens Transformed</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In CAGUL, we select the <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> least attended visual tokens to encode unlearning-specific objectives via linear transformation using <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we study the influence of the number of visual tokens transformed on the overall performance of CAGUL by varying <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{100,200,400,600,800,1000\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>100,200,400,600,800</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{100,200,400,600,800,1000\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We report CAGUL performance on the forget set <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and non-private retain set <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{np}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{np}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S7.F2" title="In Computational Efficiency. ‣ 7.1 General Unlearning Performance ‣ 7 Experimental Results ‣ Cross-Modal Attention Guided Unlearning in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We omit the results on <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, MME, and POPE as all values of <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> achieve the same metrics on these sets.
Our results show peak performance for both forget and retain metrics when <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. We observe a minor dip in performance when transforming the <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> = <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>600</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">600</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, but CAGUL generally achieves similar performance across different <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07567v1/figures/size.png" id="S7.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/311;" width="698" height="311" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Influence of forget set size</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>Influence of Forget Set Size</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also study the effect of the size of forget set <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, which relates to the number of individuals participating in <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. We vary <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{M}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{M}|</annotation></semantics></math> across {<math id="S7.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20,40,60,80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20,40,60,80</annotation></semantics></math>} with corresponding dataset statistics shown in <a href="#S6.T1" title="In 6.1 Dataset ‣ 6 Experimental Setup ‣ Cross-Modal Attention Guided Unlearning in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and report forget and retain metrics in <a href="#S7.F3" title="In 7.2 Influence of Number of Visual Tokens Transformed ‣ 7 Experimental Results ‣ Cross-Modal Attention Guided Unlearning in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
We observe a decreasing trend for the forget metrics on <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and an increasing trend for the retain performance on <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{np}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{np}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{M}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{M}|</annotation></semantics></math> becomes larger. This result suggests that unlearning with a larger forget set size facilitates more effective forgetting of private data and retaining of non-private data, most likely due to the increased size of the training subset used to learn visual token encoder weights.</p>
</div>
